   "html_text": "Finally, consider Bence Nanay’s (2023) eliminativist view (which provides a “unified” theory of imagination insofar as it posits there is no such thing as imagination). Nanay starts from the observation that concepts encoded in ordinary language often fail to pick out what actually exists—for example, folk biological concepts like “lily” and “beech tree” fail to pick out real biological categories, and scientists aim to replace these with classifications that pick out genuine natural kinds. He a",
